<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Islamabad</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Islamabad is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, a planned city built from the early 1960s at the foot of the Margalla Hills, next to Rawalpindi. Chosen under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayub-khan">Ayub Khan</a> to replace <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a> as capital, it was laid out by the Greek planner Constantinos Doxiadis in sectors with green belts. It is home to the Faisal Mosque, one of the largest mosques in the world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/islamabad.jpg" alt="Islamabad" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fassifarooq · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Faisal_Mosque,_Islamabad_III.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Islamabad lies about 540 metres above sea level at the northern edge of the Pothohar Plateau, at the foot of the Margalla Hills, in the northern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> region. It forms a conurbation with the older Gakhar city of Rawalpindi, to its south; the two are commonly called the Twin Cities. The city is 120 km southwest of Muzaffarabad, 185 km east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a> and 295 km northwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>. It covers 906 square kilometres, and a further Specified Area of 2,717 square kilometres runs into the hills. The southern part is an undulating plain drained by the Kurang River, on which the Rawal Dam stands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Pothohar Plateau is considered one of the earliest sites of human settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Stone Age artefacts dating from 100,000 to 500,000 years ago have been found there, among them rudimentary stones from the terraces of the Soan River. Excavations by Abdul Ghafoor Lone found relics and human skulls dating back to 5000 BCE, which indicate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> peoples on the banks of the Soan, who later formed small communities around 3000 BCE. Remnants of Aryan, Soanian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley">Indus Valley</a> cultures, and Buddhist remains, can still be found in the region.</p><p>Later buildings include Pharwala Fort, built by the Gakhars in the 15th century, and Rawat Fort of the 16th century, which holds the grave of their chief Sultan Sarang Khan. The Mughal commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/man-singh-i">Raja Man Singh</a> turned the village of Saidpur into a place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> worship and built ponds there, and the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> built the shrine of Bari Imam. The modern city was built as a planned capital in the 1960s and established in 1967, replacing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a>. The Greek architect Constantinos Apostolou Doxiadis drew up its master plan, which divided the city into eight zones: administrative, commercial, educational, industrial, diplomatic, residential, rural and green.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The name means City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, from the Arabic Islam and the Persian suffix -abad. Residential sectors are each identified by a letter and a number and cover about 2 km by 2 km. The city is administered federally as the Islamabad Capital Territory, and its parks and forests include Margalla Hills National Park and Shakarparian. The Faisal Mosque, completed in 1986 to a design by the Turkish architect Vedat Dalokay, was named after King Faisal bin Abdul Aziz of Saudi Arabia, who financed it, and can hold up to 74,000 worshippers. The Islamabad Museum holds relics of the Gandhara period.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Islamabad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The fourth Caliph moved to the Fazl Mosque in London in 1984, and the fifth moved the headquarters to <b>Islamabad</b>, Tilford, England, in 2019.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ahmadiyya">Ahmadiyya · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The façade of the Iraqi embassy in Beijing includes a replica of the gate, and those in Amman and <b>Islamabad</b> evoke it.</blockquote><a href="/work/ishtar-gate">Ishtar Gate · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
